## What?
As discovered by @ramonjd's agent, there's a very niche bug in trunk - when 'allowedBlocks' is the only supported 'Advanced' option for a block and the block inspector has tabs, then 'Manage allowed blocks' won't be shown.

## Why?
There's some logic that checks whether the 'advanced' area has fills for its slot and some other slots that are shown in the panel. When there are none, the panel and the whole settings tab is not shown. This code didn't take into account that the `allowedBlocks` feature uses its own slotfill called `PrivateInspectorControlsAllowedBlocks`, it wasn't counting those fills.

## How?
The fix is to count the `PrivateInspectorControlsAllowedBlocks` in the visibility logic for the advanced panel.

After this change there's an additional fix needed to make sure 'Manage allowed blocks' doesn't show up for patterns.

## Testing Instructions
1. Register a contrived block like this using the browser console
```js
wp.blocks.registerBlockType( 'test/allowed-blocks-container', {
	apiVersion: 3,
	title: 'Allowed blocks repro',
	category: 'design',
	supports: {
		listView: true,
		allowedBlocks: true,
		layout: true,
		anchor: false,
		customClassName: false,
		customCSS: false,
		html: false,
	},
	edit() {
		const props = wp.blockEditor.useInnerBlocksProps(
			wp.blockEditor.useBlockProps()
		);
		return wp.element.createElement( 'div', props );
	},
	save() {
		return wp.element.createElement(
			'div',
			wp.blockEditor.useBlockProps.save(),
			wp.element.createElement( wp.blockEditor.InnerBlocks.Content )
		);
	},
} );
```
2. Insert the 'Allowed blocks repro' block into the editor
3. In the Settings tab, see it has an 'Advanced' panel
4. Repeat the same steps against trunk and see it has no 'Advanced' panel and no 'Settings' tab.

